 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Scale Variation:** Off-the-shelf depth estimators often produce depth maps with arbitrary scales, requiring refitting against a common prior to prevent incorrect geometry [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Invalid Priors:** Depth maps that cannot be refitted (e.g., due to severe occlusion or estimation errors) must be discarded to prevent introducing noise [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Quality Impact:** In some instances, incorporating monocular depth predictions did not noticeably improve reconstruction quality and was harmful [S2].
+*   **Refitting Failure:** Depth maps that fail the refitting process against the prior point cloud are discarded [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Harmful Effects:** In some cases, monocular depth supervision was found to be harmful to reconstruction quality [S2].
+*   **Noise:** Regularization terms involving depth can be noisy [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Refined Depth for Geometry Supervision](19001-refined-depth-for-geometry-supervision.md)
-A lightweight depth-regularization objective used during per-scene training to refine reconstructed geometry, specifically improving local sharpness and depth consistency without sacrificing efficiency. It sets itself apart from standard photometric losses by explicitly leveraging rendered depths and input images to derive a geometry-supervision loss ($L_{refD}$), which is combined with the original training loss to yield more geometrically faithful surfaces with fast convergence. It is chosen when higher geometric fidelity is needed after initial initialization. [Refined Depth for Geometry Supervision](19001-refined-depth-for-geometry-supervision.md)
+Refined Depth for Geometry Supervision is a lightweight depth-regularization objective used during per-scene training to refine reconstructed geometry, specifically improving local sharpness and depth consistency without sacrificing efficiency. It sets itself apart from standard photometric losses by explicitly leveraging rendered depths and input images to derive a geometry-supervision loss ($L_{refD}$), which is combined with the original training loss to yield more geometrically faithful surfaces with fast convergence.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds overhead for loading depth maps and performing refitting per frame [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing depth maps for all training images [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Results in better geometry and a reduced number of floaters [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Leverages off-the-shelf monocular depth estimators to provide strong geometric priors without requiring multi-view stereo or explicit 3D scans [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Dependent on the quality of the off-the-shelf depth estimation model. Depth maps that fail refitting are discarded [S1, unofficial]. In some instances, it did not noticeably improve reconstruction quality and was harmful [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When geometry is poor or floaters are present, and external depth priors are available [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When depth maps are unavailable or when the scene has large scale variations that prevent consistent refitting [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds overhead for monocular depth inference and refitting per iteration. The refitting step requires processing the prior point cloud.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for depth maps in the dataset directory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Sources report mixed results. One source notes it results in "better geometry and reduced number of floaters" [S1, unofficial]. Another source found that "this additional depth loss in most instances did not noticeably improve reconstruction quality and in some cases was harmful" [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Can reduce floaters and improve geometric fidelity when successful [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Can be harmful to reconstruction quality in some cases [S2]. Results are not conclusive [S2].
+*   **When to use:** When floaters are a significant issue and other methods (like image-specific prompts) are not available or effective [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** When reconstruction quality is already high and depth supervision introduces noise or harm [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from techniques to improve Gaussian Splatting and radiance field reconstruction, depth supervision utilizes off-the-shelf depth estimation models (such as Depth-Anything) to provide geometric priors [S1, unofficial]. The method enforces that the depth rendered from the reference view matches the depth predicted by a pre-trained monocular depth network (e.g., MiDaS) for the input image [S2].
+*   **Origin:** The technique is discussed in the context of Gaussian Splatting optimization [S1, unofficial] and RealFusion [S2].
+*   **History:** It was explored as a way to improve geometry in single-view or multi-view reconstruction pipelines by leveraging pre-trained monocular depth networks like MiDaS [37] [S2].
+*   **Usage:** Used in 3D reconstruction pipelines to constrain the location of surfaces [S1, unofficial].
 
